A)
The menu planning requirements were implemented in accordance with the task description.
The schedule must contain at least one vegetarian option daily, must not feature identical main components
on consecutive days, and should achieve a high level of shared ingredients for maximum efficiency.
This was accomplished by implementing the constraints atLeastOneVegetarianPerDay,
noIdenticalMainComponentsConsecutiveDays, and maximizeSharedIngredientsSameDay. Initially, the planner exhibited
a noticeable behavior: it generated a schedule where both menu options on the same day were identical.
Furthermore, variety was heavily restricted because the algorithm focused on only a few dishes to perfectly
satisfy the rule regarding shared ingredients.

To counteract this behavior, the noIdenticalMainComponentsSameDay constraint was introduced, introducing variance
in the two menu options on a given day. However, because the planner lacked a strict prohibition
on repetitions across the entire scheduling period, it chose the easiest mathematical path: it selected four
specific dishes and rotated them alternately over the 30 days. Since the algorithm does not inherently
recognize monotony, two constraints were implemented on both a micro and macro level. The antiMonotony constraint
blocks dishes for a defined number of days after their use, preventing short-term repetitions and rigid patterns.
On the macro level, limitMealFrequency regulates the maximum appearance of any single dish per month to stop the
formation of repetitive blocks. The interplay between these two constraints is essential; otherwise, the planner
would either create blocks or attempt to fulfill the required frequency of a dish as quickly as possible in
immediate succession.

B)
When extending the program to incorporate cost minimization, the solver's behavior changes. Activating
minimizeCosts increases the number of vegetarian dishes from 36 to 39 per month. The planner replaces
protein-heavy dishes with meatless alternatives, as meat and fish components drive up ingredient costs.
Additionally, the relatively expensive dish 11 is completely eliminated from the schedule. The number of pure
veggie days (where both options are vegetarian) increases from 6 to 9 days. Without the strict boundaries of
antiMonotony and limitMealFrequency, the number of vegetarian meals would jump to 45, and the remaining protein
options would consist solely of dish 22, as it represents the cheapest protein component. However, such an
outcome contradicts the goal of providing a diverse and appealing menu variety.

C)
Transitioning the model to profit maximization reveals an opposite trend: the number of pure veggie days drops
back to 6 days. Furthermore, higher-cost dishes — such as the previously eliminated dish 11 — are reintroduced to
the schedule. An interesting phenomenon occurs with the unpopular dish 22, which is selected a total of 7 times
despite its low price multiplier.Although this dish is unpopular, it is selected 7 times by the solver simply because
it represents a highly cost-effective protein option. It allows the algorithm to satisfy the required protein variety
efficiently without having to rely on more expensive meat-based dishes.

The weighting of the soft constraints establishes a mathematical hierarchy that defines which rule violations are
penalized more severely than others. For the solver's functionality, the absolute numerical values are secondary;
the critical factor is the relative ratio between the levels. The penalty for a higher-level rule must always be
set high enough so that it cannot be bypassed by the accumulated violations of multiple lower-level rules.
Mathematically, this prioritization could be accurately represented using a different scale. As long as the
hierarchical order remains intact, the algorithm is guaranteed to process the constraints strictly according
to their predefined priority, never sacrificing a core structural rule for minor optimizations at the cent level.
